        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: trītūrō, trītūrāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "trītūrō, trītūrāre — \"to thresh\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In āreā servī frūmentum trītūrant. On the threshing floor the slaves thresh the grain."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "trītūrō, trītūrāre, trītūrāvī, trītūrātum, from trītūra 'threshing'. Oxen often trod the grain out. A late word; earlier writers say frūmentum terere."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "trītūrō is built on trītūra 'threshing' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short), which comes from terō 'to rub'. English triturate 'to grind to powder' comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
