          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "palea, paleae — \"chaff; straw\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Palea levis est et ā ventō portātur. Chaff is light and is carried by the wind."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "First declension, feminine: palea, paleae f. The husks left after threshing, used for fodder and bedding. English keeps palea as a term in botany."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "palea is inherited from PIE pel- 'flour, dust' (EtymDB), the root also seen in pulvis 'dust' and pollen. French paille 'straw' comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
